- https://aws.github.io/aws-lakeformation-best-practices/data-sharing/general-data-sharing/
- https://aws.amazon.com/fr/blogs/big-data/navigating-multi-account-deployments-in-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-a-governance-first-approach/
- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/hybrid-cross-account.html
- https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html


Yes. The right design is to make the management backend own the entire **publish pipeline**, while publishers only interact with an **eligible source tables** picker. In other words: publishers create Glue tables in their own account, your backend builds and maintains a curated inventory of tables they are allowed to publish, and when they select one, the backend runs the SMUS/DataZone automation so the resulting asset becomes discoverable to consumers. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/create-glue-data-source.html)

## Data model

Use a backend-owned table such as `eligible_source_tables` in RDS as the UI’s source of truth. Each row should represent one producer Glue table that is technically reachable and governance-approved for publication. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html)

Suggested columns:
- `publisher_account_id`, `publisher_principal_id`, `project_id`, `environment_id`
- `source_region`, `source_database`, `source_table`
- `share_status`, `resource_link_status`, `inventory_status`, `publish_status`
- `glue_table_arn`, `resource_link_name`, `data_source_id`, `inventory_asset_id`
- `last_sync_at`, `owner_tag`, `eligible_boolean`, `eligibility_reason`  
This lets the UI filter to “tables visible to this publisher and ready for asset creation,” while keeping enough IDs for your automation steps. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/APIReference/API_CreateDataSource.html)

## Access model

Publishers should authenticate only to your application. They should not call SMUS/DataZone publish APIs themselves, and ideally they should not browse producer Glue catalogs live from the frontend either. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-publishing.html)

Your app backend should enforce authorization like this:
- map the logged-in publisher to one or more producer accounts/projects,
- query `eligible_source_tables`,
- return only rows where `publisher_principal_id` or `publisher_account_id` matches that publisher,
- allow submit only for rows with `eligible_boolean = true`.  
This gives you app-level tenant isolation, while AWS remains the control plane for technical discovery and publication. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/create-glue-data-source.html)

## AWS roles

You need three main service-side roles:

| Role | Purpose | Key capabilities |
|---|---|---|
| Producer onboarding/share role | In producer account; prepares tables for discovery in M | Lake Formation cross-account grants on DB/table; optional share automation.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html) |
| Management discovery role | In account M; refreshes `eligible_source_tables` | Read shared Glue metadata, list resource links, run DataZone/SMUS data-source discovery.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-source-glue.html) |
| Management publish role | In account M / SMUS project context | Create/run data sources, update inventory metadata, publish inventory assets.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/APIReference/API_CreateDataSource.html) |

For Lake Formation-managed Glue assets, AWS states the publishing project role must have `DESCRIBE`, `DESCRIBE GRANTABLE`, `SELECT`, and `SELECT GRANTABLE` on the relevant database/table. That permission set belongs to the backend/project role used for publication, not to the end-user publisher in your UI. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/grant-access-to-glue-asset.html)

## Recommended flow

Use this lifecycle for every table:

1. Publisher creates a Glue table in producer account P. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/grant-access-to-glue-asset.html)
2. Producer-side automation or admin marks it as publishable and grants cross-account Lake Formation access to account M. For named-resource sharing, Lake Formation commonly requires `DESCRIBE` on the database and `SELECT`/`DESCRIBE` on tables, often with grant option where downstream grantability is needed. [amazonaws](https://www.amazonaws.cn/en/blog-selection/configure-cross-region-table-access-with-the-aws-glue-catalog-and-aws-lake-formation/)
3. Account M accepts the RAM share if required and creates resource links if you use the shared-catalog pattern. [blog.pesky](https://blog.pesky.moe/posts/2025-08-12-glue-region/)
4. Management discovery job updates `eligible_source_tables`, either from shared Glue/resource links or by creating/running a Glue data source in DataZone/SMUS. AWS documents that Glue data sources import technical metadata into project inventory and can optionally publish on import. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-source-glue.html)
5. Publisher opens your UI, searches/selects one eligible table.
6. Backend validates ownership, status, and idempotency, then performs publish automation.
7. Published asset becomes visible to domain consumers in the SMUS/DataZone catalog, while inventory-only assets remain visible only within the project until published. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/publishing-data.html)

## API sequence

A practical backend sequence is:

- `refreshEligibleTables(publisherScope)`  
  - list shared/resource-linked Glue tables or query DataZone/SMUS project inventory,  
  - upsert `eligible_source_tables`. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-source-glue.html)

- `listEligibleTablesForPublisher(userId)`  
  - query RDS, not AWS live.  
  - return rows with friendly labels, schema metadata, and status. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-publishing.html)

- `createAssetFromTable(selectionId, businessMetadata)`  
  1. load row from `eligible_source_tables`,  
  2. verify `eligible_boolean`,  
  3. if no inventory asset exists, create or run Glue data source for the corresponding DB scope,  
  4. wait for ingestion completion,  
  5. update business metadata/forms if needed,  
  6. publish the inventory asset or use publish-on-import if acceptable,  
  7. persist resulting catalog asset identifiers and status. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/manually-start-data-source-run.html)

For automation, prefer **inventory first, publish second** instead of immediate `publishOnImport=true` when publishers need to add business metadata or approval context before consumers discover the asset. AWS explicitly notes that imported assets can stay in project inventory first, then be published later, and only published assets are discoverable by other domain users. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/manually-start-data-source-run.html)

## UI states

Your picker should expose explicit statuses so the workflow is clear:

| UI status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Draft in producer | Table exists in Glue but has not been shared/onboarded yet. |
| Shared to management | Lake Formation/RAM step complete; table is technically reachable.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html) |
| In inventory | Table metadata has been imported into SMUS/DataZone project inventory.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-source-glue.html) |
| Ready to publish | Required metadata present; backend can create/publish asset.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-publishing.html) |
| Published | Discoverable by consumers in catalog.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fr_fr/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/publishing-data-asset.html) |

This makes the app understandable to publishers: they are not “creating SMUS assets,” they are “submitting a Glue table for catalog publication.” That matches your actual responsibility split. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fr_fr/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/publishing-data-asset.html)

## Design choices

I would strongly recommend these choices:

- Use **RDS as the picker cache/system of workflow state**, not Glue live queries at page render time.
- Use **backend-only publish roles**, never end-user AWS permissions for publishing. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fr_fr/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/publishing-data-asset.html)
- Use **Glue data source -> project inventory -> publish** as the normal path, because it aligns with how SMUS/DataZone model discoverability. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/create-glue-data-source.html)
- Keep **publish-on-import disabled by default** if publishers must add metadata, ownership text, subscription terms, or internal approval before consumers can discover assets. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-source-glue.html)

## Minimal schema

A minimal relational design could be:

- `publishers(id, identity_key, producer_account_id, default_project_id)`
- `eligible_source_tables(id, publisher_id, account_id, region, database_name, table_name, inventory_asset_id, data_source_id, status, eligible, last_sync_at, unique(account_id, region, database_name, table_name))`
- `asset_publication_requests(id, eligible_source_table_id, requested_by, requested_at, business_name, business_description, status, published_asset_id, error_message)`
- `publication_events(id, request_id, step, status, payload_json, created_at)`

That gives you strong idempotency, auditability, and retry support for the automation path. The `publication_events` table is especially useful because DataZone/SMUS ingestion and publishing are multi-step and asynchronous. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/APIReference/API_CreateDataSource.html)

## Guardrails

Add these validations before publishing:
- table still exists in Glue/shared inventory,
- publisher is still authorized for that source row,
- backend/project role still has required Lake Formation permissions,
- no already-published active asset exists for the same source unless versioning/update is intended,
- required business metadata is complete. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-publishing.html)

The clean mental model is: **Glue table is the source object, inventory asset is the staging object, catalog asset is the discoverable object**. Your backend owns the transitions between those states. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fr_fr/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/publishing-data-asset.html)


