Yes — here is a concrete design that you can implement. The core principle is: the publisher selects a **source Glue table** in your app, but only the management backend performs the **inventory ingestion + publication** steps that make the asset discoverable to consumers in SMUS/DataZone. Project inventory assets are private to project members until explicitly published, and only published assets become visible in the catalog. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/publishing-data-asset.html)

## Sequence flow

A practical end-to-end sequence looks like this:

1. Publisher creates a Lake Formation-managed Glue table in producer account P. SMUS-managed Glue assets require Lake Formation-managed tables, and the publishing project role must later have `DESCRIBE`, `DESCRIBE GRANTABLE`, `SELECT`, and `SELECT GRANTABLE` on the database/table. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/grant-access-to-glue-asset.html)
2. Producer-side automation shares the approved database/table to account M using Lake Formation named-resource sharing; for external accounts, grantable permissions are part of the cross-account model, and only the data lake admin in the consumer account can initially see/use the shared resource until it is delegated further. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-named-resource.html)
3. In account M, accept the AWS RAM share if needed and create Glue resource links for the shared database/table. Lake Formation notes that resource-link permissions and target-table permissions are separate: granting `DESCRIBE` on a resource link does not itself grant access to the linked target. [noise.getoto](https://noise.getoto.net/2022/01/19/securely-share-your-data-across-aws-accounts-using-aws-lake-formation/)
4. Management discovery job reads the shared/resource-linked metadata and upserts your RDS row in `eligible_source_tables`. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/publishing-data.html)
5. Publisher opens your UI and selects a row from `eligible_source_tables`.
6. Backend validates authorization, ensures the SMUS/DataZone project role has required Lake Formation rights on the external Glue DB/table, then either creates/runs a Glue data source or maps to an existing inventory asset. AWS states external Glue databases used by a SMUS project require explicit Lake Formation permissions on the project IAM role. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/lake-formation-permissions-for-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio.html)
7. Backend enriches inventory metadata if needed, then publishes the asset from project inventory into the catalog. Published assets become discoverable to other domain users; inventory-only assets do not. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/publishing-data-asset.html)

## Sequence diagram

Here is the logical sequence in Mermaid form:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Pub as Publisher User
    participant UI as Management UI
    participant API as Management Backend (Account M)
    participant RDS as RDS Eligible Tables
    participant LFp as Lake Formation / Glue (Producer P)
    participant LFm as Lake Formation / Glue (Mgmt M)
    participant DZ as SMUS/DataZone Project

    Pub->>LFp: Create Glue table in producer account
    LFp-->>API: Optional event/onboarding trigger
    API->>LFp: Verify table metadata + governance eligibility
    API->>LFp: Grant cross-account LF share to account M
    LFp-->>LFm: Share DB/table via RAM/LF
    API->>LFm: Accept share / create resource links
    API->>RDS: Upsert eligible_source_tables row

    Pub->>UI: Open "Create asset from table"
    UI->>API: List eligible tables for this publisher
    API->>RDS: Query eligible rows by publisher scope
    RDS-->>API: Return eligible rows
    API-->>UI: Return filtered table list

    Pub->>UI: Select table + enter business metadata
    UI->>API: Submit publication request
    API->>RDS: Lock row / create publication request
    API->>DZ: Create or run Glue data source / resolve inventory asset
    DZ-->>API: Inventory asset available
    API->>DZ: Update metadata / publish asset
    DZ-->>API: Catalog asset published
    API->>RDS: Mark request published + save asset IDs
    API-->>UI: Success response
```

The key boundary is that the UI never calls AWS directly for publishing; it only operates on backend-managed rows and requests. That keeps AWS permissions centralized and auditable. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/publishing-data.html)

## IAM and LF grants

You should model permissions in four layers:

| Layer | Principal | Needed access |
|---|---|---|
| Producer governance | Producer LF admin/share role | Grant cross-account DB/table permissions from P to M.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-named-resource.html) |
| Consumer discovery | Management discovery role in M | Read shared metadata, manage resource links, refresh RDS inventory.  [noise.getoto](https://noise.getoto.net/2022/01/19/securely-share-your-data-across-aws-accounts-using-aws-lake-formation/) |
| SMUS publish context | SMUS/DataZone project IAM role in M | Access external Glue database/table with explicit Lake Formation grants.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/lake-formation-permissions-for-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio.html) |
| App workflow | Management backend publish role | Call DataZone/SMUS APIs to create/run data sources and publish assets.  [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/APIReference/API_CreateDataSource.html) |

For the producer-to-management Lake Formation share, the minimum named-resource pattern is typically:
- on the database: `DESCRIBE`,
- on the table: `DESCRIBE`, `SELECT`,
- include grantable permissions if the consumer-side admin/project must further delegate access. AWS explicitly states cross-account named-resource sharing to external accounts uses grantable permissions, and the consumer account admin then delegates to local principals. [docs.amazonaws](https://docs.amazonaws.cn/en_us/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-table-permissions.html)

For the SMUS project role on an external database, AWS says you must explicitly attach Lake Formation permissions for that role when publishing assets from a Glue database outside the project. For managed Glue assets, the publishing project role must have `DESCRIBE` and `DESCRIBE GRANTABLE` on the database, plus `DESCRIBE`, `SELECT`, `DESCRIBE GRANTABLE`, and `SELECT GRANTABLE` on the table. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/lake-formation-permissions-for-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio.html)

Also remember that if you create a resource link in M, granting `DESCRIBE` on the resource link lets a local principal see the link, but does not grant permissions on the underlying shared target. Those target permissions must still exist separately. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-link-permissions.html)

## Backend APIs

I would define these backend operations:

- `POST /onboarding/share-table`
  - input: publisher account, region, db, table
  - action: validate table exists and is LF-governed; initiate/verify share to M; create resource links if you automate that. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html)

- `POST /inventory/refresh`
  - input: publisher scope or account scope
  - action: enumerate shared/resource-linked tables; upsert `eligible_source_tables`. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/data-source-glue.html)

- `GET /publishable-tables`
  - input: authenticated publisher
  - action: return only rows mapped to that publisher from RDS. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/publishing-data.html)

- `POST /asset-publications`
  - input: `eligible_source_table_id` + business metadata
  - action: create idempotent publication request, create/run data source if needed, resolve inventory asset, publish inventory asset, persist result. DataZone documents `CreateDataSource`, and data-source runs can also be started manually/through automation flows. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/APIReference/API_CreateDataSource.html)

- `GET /asset-publications/{id}`
  - action: poll publication status from RDS/workflow state.

This split keeps the table picker fast because the UI reads from RDS, not from live cross-account Glue calls on every page load. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/APIReference/API_CreateDataSource.html)

