Yes — here’s exactly how P1’s Lake Formation share works and how M sees/accesses that table.

We’ll cover the producer steps (P1), the consumer acceptance (M), and what you must set so your management app can list and use the shared table.
## 1) Producer (P1): give cross-account Lake Formation permissions to account M
In P1’s account, the table must be Lake Formation governed, and you grant access to the external account M ID. You do this in Lake Formation console or API with a GRANT operation. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html)

Steps in console (P1):

1. Open Lake Formation → “Data lake permissions” → Grant.
2. For **Principals**, choose **External accounts**, enter account M ID.
3. For **Database**, select the database containing your table.
4. For **Tables**, choose the specific table (or a few selected tables, or “All tables”).
5. Set permissions:
   - on DB: `DESCRIBE` (+ `GRANT` option if you want the consumer to further grant),
   - on table: `SELECT`, `DESCRIBE` (+ `GRANT` option if needed).
6. Confirm and apply the grant. [aws.amazon](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/securely-share-your-data-across-aws-accounts-using-aws-lake-formation/)

API sketch:

```python
import boto3
lf = boto3.client("lakeformation", region="us-east-1")

lf.grant_permissions(
    CatalogId="P1_ACCOUNT_ID",
    Principal={"DataLakePrincipals": [{"ARN": "arn:aws:iam::M_ACCOUNT_ID:root"}]},
    Resource={"Database": {"Name": "prod_db"}},
    Permissions=["DESCRIBE"],
    GrantablePermissions=["DESCRIBE"]
)

lf.grant_permissions(
    CatalogId="P1_ACCOUNT_ID",
    Principal={"DataLakePrincipals": [{"ARN": "arn:aws:iam::M_ACCOUNT_ID:root"}]},
    Resource={"Table": {"DatabaseName": "prod_db", "Name": "my_table"}},
    Permissions=["SELECT", "DESCRIBE"],
    GrantablePermissions=["SELECT", "DESCRIBE"]
)
```

AWS RAM is automatically used under the hood to share the catalog resources. As of early 2026, Lake Formation uses a single RAM resource share for unlimited tables, with wildcard patterns rather than individual associations. [aws.amazon](https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/aws-lake-formation-cross-account-sharing/)

EDIA: Only LF admins can see the share; end users in the consumer account are later given permissions by the consumer LF admin. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html)
## 2) Consumer (M): accept the RAM share and see the table
Access from M depends on whether P1 and M are in the same AWS Organization. [aws.amazon](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/securely-share-your-data-across-aws-accounts-using-aws-lake-formation/)
### If same organization
- If P1 and M are in the same AWS Organization/OU:
  - If P1 and M are in the same AWS Organization or organizational unit (OU), the shared resource is immediately available M-side under the shared tab in Lake Formation. [youtube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S-Mdcmq6oPM)
  - RAM assume-accept flow is typically automatic for orgs; M may not need manual acceptance.
- M will see the shared database and tables under Lake Formation → “Data permissions” and the “Shared with me” section.
### If different organizations
1. In M, go to RAM → **Shared with me** → **Resource shares**.
2. Accept the incoming Lake Formation resource share from P1. [aws.amazon](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/securely-share-your-data-across-aws-accounts-using-aws-lake-formation/)
3. After acceptance, the shared DB/table appears in M’s Lake Formation console.

At this point, M does not yet automatically get full browsing access for all its users; first, M’s Lake Formation admin must grant local permissions.
## 3) M: grant permissions to your backend/project roles
By default, only the Lake Formation admin in M can use the shared resources. To let your backend and SMUS project role access them, you must explicitly grant permissions on the shared DB/table to:

- your **management discovery role** (for listing tables),
- your **SMUS project IAM role** (for reading and publishing as a managed asset). [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/grant-access-to-glue-asset.html)

In M, as LF admin:

1. Lake Formation → Data lake permissions → Grant.
2. Principals: IAM role ARN(s), e.g.:
   - `arn:aws:iam::M_ACCOUNT_ID:role/management-discovery-role`
   - `arn:aws:iam::M_ACCOUNT_ID:role/smus-project-role`
3. Resource: the shared database and table.
4. Permissions (same as above):
   - DB: `DESCRIBE`
   - Table: `SELECT`, `DESCRIBE` (and `GRANT` if needed). [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html)

For managed Glue assets in SMUS, AWS explicitly states the publishing project role must have `DESCRIBE`, `DESCRIBE GRANTABLE`, `SELECT`, and `SELECT GRANTABLE` on the database/table. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/grant-access-to-glue-asset.html)
## 4) How M “sees” the table
Thanks to Lake Formation’s cross-account feature, the shared metadata appears in M as if it were local:

- In Lake Formation console, under Databases, you’ll see the shared DB and tables. [aws.amazon](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/securely-share-your-data-across-aws-accounts-using-aws-lake-formation/)
- In Athena (in M), you can query `SELECT * FROM prod_db.my_table` as long as the query principal has LF permissions. [blog.pesky](https://blog.pesky.moe/posts/2025-08-12-glue-region/)
- In your backend, using boto3 with the discovery role, you can list tables using `glue:GetTables` on the shared DB or query roster views via Lake Formation.

From M, the guidance is that “users and Glue jobs can query and join tables across multiple accounts” while benefiting from LF-level/column protections. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html)

Practically, the backend runs something like:

```python
glue = boto3.client("glue", region="us-east-1")

tables = glue.get_tables(
    DatabaseName="prod_db",  # the shared DB
    MaxResults=100
)
```

If `get_tables` is called using a role that has LF permissions on that DB/table in M, it returns the shared tables from P1. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html)
## 5) Optional: create resource links in M
If you want the shared catalog to appear in other regions, or want a consistent local naming pattern, you can create resource links:

1. In M, open Lake Formation.
2. Under Databases, create a **resource link** for the shared database/table. [aws.github](https://aws.github.io/aws-lakeformation-best-practices/data-sharing/general-data-sharing/)
3. Use that link name in downstream access (e.g., Athena, SMUS data sources, your app). [blog.pesky](https://blog.pesky.moe/posts/2025-08-12-glue-region/)

Note: permissions on the resource link and on the underlying target are separate. You must grant permissions both:
- on the target in P1 to M (cross-account grant),
- on the resource link in M to local roles. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-link-permissions.html)
## 6) How your app/UI discovers the table
Your management backend uses the discovery role in M to enumerate shared tables:

- Filter to databases/tables belonging to each publisher (you can tag or encode `publisher_id` in metadata tags/parameters on the DB/table).
- Store those rows in RDS into `eligible_source_tables`. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html)
- UI then just reads from RDS and shows only rows for that publisher.

This avoids live cross-account `get_tables` per user session and keeps authorization centralized in your backend. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/publishing-data.html)
## 7) Steps summary for P1 → M
**Producer side (P1):**
- Ensure table is Lake Formation governed.
- Grant cross-account permissions to M: `DESCRIBE` on DB, `SELECT` + `DESCRIBE` on table.
- Optionally grant `GRANT` options if M must redelegate.

**Consumer side (M):**
1. Accept RAM share (if not same org).
2. Grant local permissions to discovery role + SMUS project role.
3. Optionally create resource links.
4. Backend reads shared metadata via Glue/Lake Formation, populates RDS. [docs.aws.amazon](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cross-account-permissions.html)

